<!-- Website teaching layer for android/data-and-di/storage-and-content-sharing.md.
     The original chapter is rendered in full; everything here is added around it.
     Checked against developer.android.com (Photo Picker, persisting media access; Storage Access Framework;
     FileProvider sharing) on 2026-10-25. -->

<!-- @opening -->
<p class="story-lead">A user picks a video. Later the app stops, and its upload worker restarts without access to that URI. Keeping the URI string did not keep its temporary permission.</p>

The storage API determines who owns the bytes and how long URI access lasts. Follow one selection through a background upload, then choose the right primitive for storage and sharing.

<!-- @after Decision Matrix :: visual -->
### Who owns it, and who chose it?

<figure class="viz"><div class="viz-grid" style="--cols:2"><div class="node is-key"><strong>The app owns it</strong><small><code>filesDir</code> / <code>cacheDir</code>, Room, DataStore: private, deleted on uninstall, no permission</small></div><div class="node"><strong>The app publishes it</strong><small>MediaStore: photos, video, audio added to shared collections</small></div><div class="node" data-state="done"><strong>The user chooses media</strong><small>Photo Picker: access to the selected items only, no library permission</small></div><div class="node" data-state="done"><strong>The user chooses a document or folder</strong><small>Storage Access Framework: a document or tree URI</small></div></div><figcaption>To give another app one of your files, use <code>FileProvider</code> with a temporary URI grant.</figcaption></figure>

<!-- @after URI Ownership and Lifetime :: animation -->
### Follow one picked video until the upload runs

<figure class="stepper" data-illustrated data-no-handoff><span class="stepper-title">Photo Picker / PickVisualMedia: temporary versus persisted access</span><div class="stage viz-cols" style="--cols:2"><div class="col"><span class="col-title">Temporary grant only</span><div class="node" data-el="t1" data-art="phone" data-art-role="client"><strong>User picks a video</strong><small>app receives <code>content://…/42</code></small></div><div class="node" data-el="t2" data-art="document" data-art-role="storage"><strong>Store the URI string</strong><small>enqueue an upload worker</small></div><div class="node" data-el="t3" data-art="phone" data-art-role="client"><strong>App process ends</strong><small>default picker access ends</small></div><div class="node" data-el="t4" data-art="key" data-art-role="storage"><strong>Worker opens the URI</strong><small><code>SecurityException</code></small></div></div><div class="col"><span class="col-title">Persisted grant</span><div class="node" data-el="p1" data-art="phone" data-art-role="client"><strong>User picks a video</strong><small>same URI</small></div><div class="node" data-el="p2" data-art="key" data-art-role="storage"><strong><code>takePersistableUriPermission()</code></strong><small>record the URI and the intended operation</small></div><div class="node" data-el="p3" data-art="phone" data-art-role="client"><strong>App process ends</strong><small>persisted access remains</small></div><div class="node" data-el="p4" data-art="document" data-art-role="storage"><strong>Worker opens the URI</strong><small>streams through <code>ContentResolver</code>, still handling revocation</small></div></div></div><script type="application/json" class="steps">[
{"label":"Pick","caption":"Both apps receive a content URI from Photo Picker (PickVisualMedia). Neither receives a file path, and neither owns the bytes.","add":["t1","p1"],"state":{"t1":"done","p1":"done"}},
{"label":"Record","caption":"The first app stores only the string. The second also takes a persistable permission, which Photo Picker URIs support, and records why it needs it.","add":["t2","p2"],"state":{"t2":"warn","p2":"done"}},
{"label":"Time passes","caption":"The app process terminates; this is not merely Activity.onStop(). Photo Picker’s default access lasts until the app stops or the device restarts; the second app requested persisted access. SAF document grants have their own lifetime rules.","add":["t3","p3"],"state":{"t3":"error","p3":"done"}},
{"label":"Upload","caption":"The first worker fails. The second opens a stream, and still treats revocation or a vanished item as an expected outcome, not a crash.","add":["t4","p4"],"state":{"t4":"error","p4":"done"}}
]</script><figcaption>If a persistable grant is not available, copy the validated bytes into app storage while the temporary grant is still valid.</figcaption></figure>

<!-- @after Production Design :: visual -->
### Ingest a file as a state machine

<figure class="viz"><div class="viz-flow"><div class="node"><strong>Selected</strong></div><div class="node"><strong>Permission recorded</strong></div><div class="node"><strong>Copying / streaming</strong><small>off the main thread</small></div><div class="node is-key"><strong>Validated</strong><small>sniffed type, byte and pixel limits</small></div><div class="node" data-state="done"><strong>Uploaded / stored</strong></div></div><figcaption>Every step can also end in failure or cancellation. Filename extensions and declared MIME types are hints, not validation.</figcaption></figure>

<!-- @after Failure Modes :: check -->
**Pick the primitive.** Decide before opening each answer.

<details><summary>Attach one photo to a chat message.</summary>

The Photo Picker. It grants access to the chosen photo only, with no media-library permission.
</details>

<details><summary>Let the user export a PDF report into a folder they choose.</summary>

The Storage Access Framework (`ACTION_CREATE_DOCUMENT`), writing through the returned URI.
</details>

<details><summary>Send a generated invoice PDF to the user's email app.</summary>

`FileProvider` for that one file, with a temporary read grant on the share intent. Never a `file://` URI.
</details>
